From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ath11k: nss: no cacheable rings when the NSS core owns them (E85)

E85. With alloc_cacheable_memory (ipq8074/ipq6018/ipq5018/qcn9074) the
REO destination and WBM2SW release rings are allocated with
dma_alloc_noncoherent and managed with host side cache maintenance in
the hal srng accessors. In NSS offload mode the consumer of exactly
these rings is the UBI32 core, which takes no part in the host CPU
cache protocol, and the host never runs the accessors for them - but
still holds the ring through a cacheable mapping, so any dirty line
writeback clobbers descriptors under the firmware. The srng flags word
sent to the firmware in the wifili init message also carries the purely
host-local HAL_SRNG_FLAGS_CACHED bit (0x20000000) for these rings.

Stock QSDK 12.2 avoids both explicitly ("Disable cached desc if NSS
offload is enabled" in dp_main.c, and the TCL twin in
dp_alloc_tx_ring_pair_by_index). Mirror it: skip the cacheable
allocation when nss is enabled.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/dp.c
@@ -256,7 +256,13 @@
 
 	ring->size = (num_entries * entry_sz) + HAL_RING_BASE_ALIGN - 1;
 
-	if (ab->hw_params.alloc_cacheable_memory) {
+	/* E85: never from cacheable memory when the ring consumer is the NSS
+	 * core - it takes no part in the host CPU cache protocol, and stock
+	 * QSDK does the same ("Disable cached desc if NSS offload is enabled"
+	 * in dp_main.c). This also keeps HAL_SRNG_FLAGS_CACHED out of the
+	 * flags word the wifili init message hands to the firmware.
+	 */
+	if (ab->hw_params.alloc_cacheable_memory && !ab->nss.enabled) {
 		/* Allocate the reo dst and tx completion rings from cacheable memory */
 		switch (type) {
 		case HAL_REO_DST:
